---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { resolvePath } from '../lib/paths';
---

<BaseLayout title="Project">
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-16">
    <p class="font-mono text-sm text-orbit tracking-widest mb-4" data-reveal="1">
      PROJECT PROPOSAL · PERCEIVE → DETECT → REPLAN
    </p>
    <h1 data-reveal="1">ORLO Assembly Agent</h1>

    <!-- Animated perceive-detect-replan loop -->
    <div class="my-12 p-8 border border-orbit/20 bg-space-950/30 rounded-none" data-reveal="2">
      <div class="flex justify-center items-center gap-6 md:gap-8">
        <div class="text-center">
          <div class="w-16 h-16 md:w-20 md:h-20 mx-auto border-2 border-orbit flex items-center justify-center animate-pulse">
            <span class="text-orbit font-mono text-sm">O1</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-orbit mt-3 font-mono">Perceive</p>
        </div>
        <div class="text-orbit font-mono text-lg">→</div>
        <div class="text-center">
          <div class="w-16 h-16 md:w-20 md:h-20 mx-auto border-2 border-orbit flex items-center justify-center animate-pulse" style="animation-delay: 0.3s;">
            <span class="text-orbit font-mono text-sm">O2</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-orbit mt-3 font-mono">Detect</p>
        </div>
        <div class="text-orbit font-mono text-lg">→</div>
        <div class="text-center">
          <div class="w-16 h-16 md:w-20 md:h-20 mx-auto border-2 border-orbit flex items-center justify-center animate-pulse" style="animation-delay: 0.6s;">
            <span class="text-thruster font-mono text-sm">Act</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-thruster mt-3 font-mono">Replan</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 data-reveal="3">The problem</h2>
    <p data-reveal="3">
      In-space assembly only matters if it's <strong>reliable</strong>. A satellite operator
      gets no benefit from on-demand CubeSat assembly if the assembly step itself is the
      least trustworthy part of the mission.
    </p>

    <p data-reveal="3">
      ORLO has already passed major milestones — it's flown, survived launch-level vibration
      testing, and has a working assembly-sequence planner (a graph-based optimization
      by labmate Leila Freitag, validated in microgravity). But underneath that success
      sit two brittle assumptions:
    </p>

    <h3 data-reveal="3">Perception is brittle</h3>
    <p data-reveal="3">
      Module identification relies on fiducial markers (visual tags) to tell the gantry robot
      which module is which and where it sits. Fiducials fail in ways that matter:
      orbital lighting swings from harsh direct sun to deep shadow far faster than Earth,
      markers are occluded by the arm or by other modules, and a damaged or misprinted marker
      simply can't be read. When a read fails, the entire assembly pipeline has no fallback.
    </p>

    <h3 data-reveal="4">Planning doesn't adapt</h3>
    <p data-reveal="4">
      The sequencer computes an assembly order in advance and executes it open-loop — no
      model of things going wrong mid-assembly. A module that doesn't fully seat, a connector
      that fails continuity check, a gantry position that drifts outside tolerance — these
      are all fatal to the current system. At best it stops and waits for a human.
    </p>

    <p data-reveal="4">
      Neither gap is ORLO-specific. Every "autonomous assembly" claim in the in-space-servicing
      literature quietly assumes nominal conditions. <strong>The research question this project
      takes on is:</strong> Can an agent perceive the real state of a modular assembly system under
      uncertainty, and use that perception to decide — in real time — whether to retry, reorder,
      or escalate, instead of just executing a fixed plan?
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal="5">Why it matters</h2>
    <p data-reveal="5">
      Large tiled structures in orbit (mirrors, solar arrays, radiators) unlock missions that
      cannot be launched fully assembled — they don't fit in any vehicle. The path to making
      that capability real depends on two things happening: (1) the hardware must be mature and
      tested, which ORLO is getting there on; and (2) the autonomous assembly software must be
      robust enough that operators trust it. Today's open-loop pipeline is not yet trustworthy
      enough. A system that notices and recovers from failures in real time is.
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal="6">What the agent will do</h2>
    <p data-reveal="6">
      The agent operates in a loop:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-base text-space-200 space-y-2" data-reveal="6">
      <li><strong>Perceive (O1):</strong> Read fiducial markers and/or learned pose estimates to build a state estimate of which module is where, along with a confidence score and an anomaly flag (none / occlusion / damage / misalignment).</li>
      <li><strong>Detect (O2):</strong> Consume the perception output plus execution telemetry (gantry torque, position error, continuity checks, vibration) and decide: is this step nominal, or is something wrong?</li>
      <li><strong>Replan (O2):</strong> If something is wrong, choose an action — retry the current step, skip it and reorder the remaining sequence, or escalate to a human operator. Execute the chosen action and loop.</li>
    </ul>

    <p data-reveal="6" class="mt-4">
      The agent treats the perception module, the existing graph planner, and the gantry interface
      as tools it can call. Its job is to reason over their outputs and decide the next action,
      rather than to re-implement any of them from scratch. A rule-based policy is the baseline;
      a learned or LLM-orchestrated policy is the stretch goal.
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal="7">Development approach</h2>
    <p data-reveal="7">
      Real hardware access is limited, so the project runs two tracks in parallel:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-base text-space-200 space-y-2" data-reveal="7">
      <li><strong>Simulation track:</strong> Build a synthetic fault-injection environment (renders of the locker with injectable faults — occluded marker, misaligned module, failed connector). Test the perceive-detect-replan loop end-to-end in simulation before touching hardware.</li>
      <li><strong>Hardware-in-the-loop track:</strong> Validate the perception model against real ORLO testbed imagery; if lab time allows, run the full agent loop against the actual gantry.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 data-reveal="8">Milestones</h2>
    <ol class="text-base text-space-200 space-y-2" data-reveal="8">
      <li>Stand up the perception module against existing ORLO imagery/CAD; establish a baseline against plain fiducial reads.</li>
      <li>Build the synthetic fault-injection environment; define the fault taxonomy.</li>
      <li>Implement the agent loop (perceive → detect → decide) against simulated faults; get a rule-based policy working end to end.</li>
      <li>Optionally, compare a learned or LLM-orchestrated policy against the baseline.</li>
      <li>Validate on real testbed data/hardware to whatever extent is possible; report where simulation diverges from reality.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 data-reveal="9">Success metrics</h2>
    <ul class="text-base text-space-200 space-y-2" data-reveal="9">
      <li>Perception accuracy and confidence calibration under injected occlusion/lighting faults</li>
      <li>Fraction of injected faults the agent correctly diagnoses</li>
      <li>Assembly completion rate and time-to-recovery on faulted runs vs. the open-loop baseline (which, by design, doesn't recover at all)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 data-reveal="10">Data and restrictions</h2>
    <p data-reveal="10">
      All data used in this project is public, synthetic, or redacted — no proprietary or
      sensitive material. The simulation environment will use ORLO's public CAD geometry,
      and real testbed imagery will be anonymized or synthetic-render augmented before publication.
      This ensures all work can be shared with collaborators and the broader community.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
